{"entries":[{"h":"Folden","m":"folded","n":"The earlier form of the participle.","pos":"pp.","refs":["34:1:10"]},{"h":"Folk","m":"people","n":"Used as a plural, its correct form, as in Old English.","pos":"sb.","refs":["41:6:5"]},{"h":"Follow on","m":"to follow","n":"The preposition is redundant in these phrases.","refs":["28:6:3"]},{"h":"Footmen","m":"footsoldiers, infantry","pos":"sb.","refs":["4:11:21","24:12:5"]},{"h":"For because","m":"because","n":"A redundant expression in which the two words mean the same; the combination makes it more forcible.","refs":["1:22:16","7:6:22"]},{"h":"For that","m":"because, inasmuch as","refs":["1:41:32","54:1:12"]},{"h":"For to","m":"in order to","refs":["1:31:18","2:16:27"]},{"h":"For all","m":"although, notwithstanding","refs":["43:21:11"]},{"h":"Forbear","m":"to be indulgent to, or patient with","pos":"v.t.","refs":["51:3:13"]},{"h":"Force","m":"physical vigour","pos":"sb.","refs":["5:34:7"]},{"h":"Forecast","m":"to devise beforehand","pos":"v.t.","refs":["27:11:24","27:11:25"]},{"h":"Foreknow","m":"to know beforehand","pos":"v.t.","refs":["45:8:29"]},{"h":"Foreknowledge","m":"previous knowledge","n":"The Greek word is the origin of 'prognostication' and foreknowledge was also used in something of this sense.","pos":"sb.","refs":["44:2:23","60:1:2"]},{"h":"Foreordained","m":"ordained beforehand","pos":"pp.","refs":["60:1:20"]},{"h":"Forepart","m":"the bow of a ship","pos":"sb.","refs":["44:27:41"]},{"h":"Forerunner","m":"a messenger sent in advance to announce another's coming","n":"A literal translation of the Greek, matching an Old English word.","pos":"sb.","refs":["58:6:20"]},{"h":"Foreship","m":"the bow of a ship","pos":"sb.","refs":["44:27:30"]},{"h":"Foretell","m":"to tell beforehand","pos":"v.t.","refs":["47:13:2"]},{"h":"Forgat","m":"forgot","n":"The old form of the past tense of 'forget'.","refs":["1:40:23"]},{"h":"Forgiven unto","m":"forgiven","n":"In Old English the verb takes a dative, so the preposition is red